r  new information and build on it and this  is an example of exactly that kind of  productivity benefit in my own  experiences in a company developing  engineered yeast the yeast were  developed over a multi-year period they  were genetically engineered to produce a  compound at high yields and the goal of  this project was to improve those yields  through genetic engineering in the early  years due to a lot of noise in the  system we would see rare large jumps in  performance with a lot of plateaus and  flat lining in between and at some point  along the way we started to get  concerned that we would never emerge  from from those flatline and we  introduced a quality program which  reduced the error measurement by six  fold what that did is make the lots of  little improvements that normally would  go undetected because they were below  the noise threshold it made those  improvements detectable and reproducible  and when you can find those improvements  and then build on them quickly you've  got an overall doubling in the pace of  discovery and development of those  strengths then the key to that is being  able to not only just detect those  results but trust them and to stack that  knowledge quickly and that's a metaphor  for science as a whole which is now a  global enterprise across many dozens of  people collaborating on the typical  product project or thousands of people  collaborating on industrial development  programs you need to share and build on  each other's work so what are these  lessons of manufacturing quality that  have been successfully also applied in a  biotech setting and how can they be  applied more broadly that's really the  foundation of what we're if in saw to  address and to embed until its top or  capabilities they're actually quite  simple and they're quite well known they  start with design the idea that if you  want to have a trustworthy result you  have to be very explicit  about how are you going to deliver that  result was the process or technology or  method you're going to use to achieve  that outcome then you measure whether  you're actually delivering on the design  specification and analyze that data to  look for deviations from expectation and  ways to improve it once you make those  improvements you share the result and  iterate cycling back to the design to  improve on the design and it's a  continuous process it's often called  continuous improvement but what that  really means is design your system  measure its performance analyze it for  improvement opportunities and then  update your organization to work better  the problem is that these methods which  are commonly applied in manufacturing  are often hard to apply an RD because  aren't econ stantly changing it's so  flexible there's so many different data  modality hard to bring that together  so what riffin is done is created a  cloud-based collaborative software  platform that embeds these principles  into its architecture and it's  completely adaptive meaning as you  change your experimental research  processes on it on an hourly basis even  the entire system updates itself and  adapts to those changes so that you can  continue to collect data and apply the  principles of cloud in essence it starts  with it boils down to those four phases  a design capability which allows you to  specify without ambiguity the  experimental methods and processes you  use it then uses those experimental  processes to provide automated context  to your data as you require it in  real-time that allows you to endow your  data with communion without having to  write down a lot of information for  typically that data is then linked  together using again the process design  is the guide for how to join data sets  together so that the whole analytical  pipeline or what in many organizations  is called an ETL layer or extract  transform layer is automated and the  outcome is a  data table that's fully structured and  ready for statistical analysis with 